.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
*.db
# THE SQLITE SIDECARS, added 2026-10-03. `*.db` above does not match them, so
# swap_terminal.db-shm and swap_terminal.db-wal showed as UNTRACKED in every
# `git status` of a tree where anything had opened the database -- which is noise
# that hides a real new file, and worse, invites a `git add -A` to commit one.
#
# A COMMITTED WAL IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY. It holds pages not yet checkpointed into
# the .db, so it can carry swap rows, addresses and amounts; and a WAL without the
# database it belongs to is unreadable, so it would be an unreviewable binary
# carrying customer data. The one .db already tracked here predates the `*.db`
# line above and is a separate question.
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite3
wallet.dat
*.log

# secrets and backups
.env
.env.*
*.bak
*.bak.*
*keypair*.json
*.gpg

# solana local validator artifacts
test-ledger/
node_modules/

# supervisor pid files and worker logs (swap_terminal/supervisor.py).
# Runtime state, never source: a pid file that outlives its process is the
# stale record that makes the next start refuse for no reason (rule 13).
swap_terminal/runtime/

# Solana keypairs are a bare JSON array of 64 integers and are named anything at
# all. `wgrc.json` -- the key to the account every swap payout in
# swap_intents.json is addressed to -- sat in the repo because the pattern above
# was `*keypair*.json` and the file is not called that. Matching key material by
# FILENAME is guesswork; tests/test_no_key_material_is_tracked.py matches it by
# SHAPE, which is what actually holds. These lines stop the known ones early.
wgrc.json
*.keypair.json
id.json

# The cross-curve DLEQ helper binary. Built with `cd tools/dleq_helper && go
# build -o dleq_helper .`; go.sum pins the module versions so a build either
# reproduces them or fails. A committed binary is a thing nobody can review and
# everybody trusts, which is the wrong trade for the one component in this tree
# whose failure mode is "the cryptography is wrong".
tools/dleq_helper/dleq_helper
.coverage

# The two other container state volumes, for the same reason as abstergo below:
# a pid file, a worker log, a regtest chain and a WAL sidecar are all runtime
# state, and a checkout that carried them would overwrite a running desk's.
runtime/regtest/*
!runtime/regtest/.gitkeep
runtime/web/*
!runtime/web/.gitkeep

# The abstergo container's state volume. swap_intents.json is a fund-releasing
# authority (docs/containerization_design_2026_10_03.md §12) and must never be
# committed: it would put live intent records in git history and, worse, a
# checkout would overwrite a running desk's copy.
runtime/abstergo/*
!runtime/abstergo/.gitkeep

# CARGO AND DFX BUILD OUTPUT, both at the repository root because that is where
# the workspace is (see Cargo.toml's header for why dfx forces it there).
#
# target/ was ignored at icp/threshold_custody/.gitignore while that crate was
# standalone, and that file is gone rather than kept: a workspace puts target/ at
# the WORKSPACE root, so the crate-level directory can no longer be created even
# by running `cargo build` from inside the crate, and an ignore line for a path
# nothing can write is a line the next reader has to test to disbelieve (rule 9).
#
# .dfx/ is canister ids, the local replica's wasm copies, and generated candid
# bindings. Ids are environment state -- the same canister has a different one on
# every fresh replica -- so committing them would make a checkout claim to know
# something only a running replica can answer.
target/
.dfx/

# THE ICP LEDGER'S INIT ARGUMENTS, generated by icp_ledger_init.py at the root.
#
# IGNORED BECAUSE IT IS ENVIRONMENT STATE, for the same reason .dfx/ above is: it
# hardcodes two account identifiers read off a running replica with
# `dfx ledger account-id`, and the same identity has a different account on every
# fresh replica. A tracked copy would claim to know something only a live replica
# can answer, and would go stale silently the first time the container is
# recreated -- which is the exact failure mode this project keeps meeting.
#
# It exists at all because DFINITY's own ICP ledger setup says, verbatim,
# "`dfx.json` does not support referring to values through environment variables.
# Values must be hardcoded in plain text." dfx's own answer to that is
# init_arg_file, a PATH in dfx.json, so the path is tracked and the file is not.
icp/icp_ledger_init.did
